<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rögnvald Kali Kolsson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1100–1158</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Saint Rögnvald</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rögnvald Kali Kolsson was earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> from about 1136 to 1158, a nephew of the murdered Earl Magnus. He won the earldom with Norwegian help and in 1137 began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-magnus-cathedral">St Magnus Cathedral</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkwall">Kirkwall</a> in his uncle's honour. He made a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1151–1153, wrote skaldic verse, and after his murder in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a> was venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rognvald-kali-kolsson.jpg" alt="Rögnvald Kali Kolsson" width="320" height="560" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Otter · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Magnus_Cathedral_-_St_Rognvald_20110523.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
